<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bön</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bon is a Tibetan religious tradition that traces its teachings to the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonpa-shenrab-miwoche">Tonpa Shenrab Miwoche</a> and to the ancient kingdom of Zhangzhung in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Early Tibetan sources use the name for priests who performed royal funerary and other rites; the organised Bon religion that took shape from the eleventh century shares much with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. It is practised today in Tibet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bon.jpg" alt="Bon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao (peace-on-earth.org) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narshi_Gonpa_Ngawa_Sichuan_China.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Little is known about the religion of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and scholars disagree about its nature. The term bon seems originally to have meant a kind of priest, among them priests of the Yarlung kings, who propitiated local spirits and guided the dead through ceremonies for a good afterlife; the most elaborate rites concerned the kings, who had special tombs. This religion was marginalised as Buddhism arrived, and Buddhist polemics against it survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</p><p>Yungdrung Bon, the Eternal Bon, is a living tradition that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in the later dissemination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The scholar Geoffrey Samuel traces both it and the Nyingma school of Buddhism to hereditary ritual practitioners who drew on Buddhist tantra and earlier court and village rituals. A later movement, New Bon, which began in eastern Tibet with the discoveries of Tulku Loden Nyingpo (1360–1385), is closely related to both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Bonpos, the followers of Bon, hold that the religion began in the kingdom of Zhangzhung around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kailash">Mount Kailash</a> and was brought from there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. They name its founder as the Buddha Tonpa Shenrab Miwoche, whose historicity no available source establishes. Tradition holds that he reached Buddhahood in a western land, Olmo Lungring, several hundred years before Sakyamuni Buddha. Its symbol, the yungdrung, a left-facing swastika, stands for indestructibility and eternity and holds a place like that of the vajra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>.</p><p>Bon teachings closely resemble those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, especially the Nyingma school. The world is seen as a place of suffering, with karma, rebirth and six realms of existence, and liberation comes from insight into an ultimate nature called the bon body. Its teachings are arranged in the Nine Ways, and its canon has a Kanjur and a Tenjur, and a large body of termas, treasure texts held to have been hidden in a time of persecution and found from the 10th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> writers tended to portray Bon in a hostile light, and early Western studies drew on their sources and treated Bon as the pre-Buddhist religion that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> overcame. The scholar David Snellgrove, who worked with Bonpo masters and translated Bonpo texts, led Western scholarship from the 1960s to treat Bon as a tradition in its own right. Some scholars, such as Snellgrove, see Bon as a heterodox form of Buddhism; John Powers writes that Bon developed as a self-conscious religious system only under Buddhist influence. Bon suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> as Tibetan Buddhism did. Its monasteries were allowed to rebuild after 1980, and Menri Monastery was refounded at Dolanji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
